1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They've been in use for over 3,000 years,

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
but they're no longer only in physical space.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
They're in cyberspace as well.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
They're crafted with sophistication or simplicity.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
But make no mistake, using one today buys you a one-way ticket

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:20,000
to the penitentiary.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Booby Traps on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:54,560
The explosion ripped through the mezzanine floor, which houses the executive suites where

9
00:00:54,561 --> 00:00:56,960
the bomb was discovered early yesterday morning.

10
00:00:56,961 --> 00:00:59,960
The FBI continued to keep this area around Kaczynski's cabin

11
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:01,700
cordoned off from the press.

12
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,100
They refused to deny or confirm reports

13
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,600
that there was an explosion in this area earlier this morning.

14
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,600
The elderly woman who opened the mailbox

15
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,740
told her husband that the bomb looked like a flashlight.

16
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:14,540
It blew up when she touched it.

17
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:19,720
Each of these stories is a little different,

18
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,380
yet each shares one thing in common.

19
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,180
The victims never saw it coming.

20
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:30,160
A booby trap is an explosive or mechanical trap that is designed

21
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:32,760
to appear totally innocent.

22
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:36,100
But when it's moved or touched by an unwary person,

23
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,100
it suddenly goes off.

24
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,680
Anything at all can be booby trapped,

25
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:46,080
or booby traps can be fabricated from just about anything

26
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:48,420
that you can get your hands on.

27
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:50,820
Dynamite wired to a car's ignition.

28
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:55,960
As the key is turned, it ignites.

29
00:01:55,961 --> 00:01:58,300
A shotgun hooked up to a doorknob.

30
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,200
When the door opens, it goes off.

31
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:07,100
Even a water faucet, rigged so when it's turned on,

32
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,100
an entire house explodes.

33
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:11,700
Bottom line is, a lot of bad people out there

34
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:15,120
with very vivid imagination.

35
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:17,520
Most booby traps are meant to kill or seriously

36
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:18,920
injure their intended victims.

37
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:28,860
Others are designed to harass and intimidate.

38
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:32,360
But whatever their purpose, they're showing up more and more,

39
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:35,400
not only on faraway battlefields,

40
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,300
but on our streets and in our workplaces,

41
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,400
even in our homes.

42
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,320
The motives are almost endless revenge,

43
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:46,220
love triangles, property protection,

44
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:50,320
homicide, extortion, drug dealing.

45
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:53,820
You name it, it runs the gamut.

46
00:02:53,821 --> 00:02:56,820
According to the latest figures from the FBI and the U.S.

47
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,820
Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, and Firearms, or ATF,

48
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:02,820
there's been a record number of bombings or attempted bombings

49
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:06,760
recorded in the United States since 1993.

50
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:09,160
That number exceeded 8,000.

51
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:13,080
10 to 15% were directly related to booby trap incidents.

52
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:17,080
That number is expected to increase.

53
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:20,080
I believe the use of booby traps in the future

54
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:22,520
is going to continue to climb.

55
00:03:22,521 --> 00:03:26,120
to Kalan. There are so many ways that you

56
00:03:26,121 --> 00:03:30,220
can devise a booby trap around technology.

57
00:03:30,221 --> 00:03:35,120
The fact that there is such easy access to information detailing the ins and outs of booby

58
00:03:35,121 --> 00:03:39,660
trap construction isn't helping. There's a lot of publications and

59
00:03:39,661 --> 00:03:46,420
manuals out there put out by various government agencies as well as clandestine type publications

60
00:03:46,421 --> 00:03:51,520
that give that type of information. But without a doubt, the internet is probably

61
00:03:51,521 --> 00:03:53,760
one of the largest information sources we see

62
00:03:53,761 --> 00:03:57,340
when people are experimenting or making explosive devices,

63
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:58,580
kids especially.

64
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,620
In the make-believe world of video games and action movies,

65
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:06,880
booby traps are edge-of-the-seat thrillers.

66
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,980
Audiences are delighted with the unique devices

67
00:04:09,981 --> 00:04:13,120
set up to protect the mummified remains of an Egyptian pharaoh

68
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:16,300
and the rich treasures hidden alongside his corpse.

69
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:23,020
In reality, the ancient Egyptians

70
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:26,820
used booby traps sparingly.

71
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:29,320
The pharaoh's builders camouflaged the entrances

72
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:31,960
to their tombs with rocks and debris

73
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:35,360
and carved curses into the entrances and on the walls

74
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:37,960
inside the tomb.

75
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:41,340
They would do things like have a meandering pattern

76
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:42,700
for the corridors.

77
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:46,680
Sometimes some of them would end in false chambers.

78
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:50,020
Some of them would be dead ends.

79
00:04:50,021 --> 00:04:52,060
Some of them would double back.

80
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:56,360
Some of these corridors were blocked with huge stones that

81
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,960
slid into place when the tomb was sealed.

82
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:02,960
But for a select number of tombs,

83
00:05:03,060 --> 00:05:05,860
ancient engineers employed several ingenious devices

84
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:10,380
to send a crystal clear message to the would-be grave robber.

85
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:13,280
One of the obvious ways of making a booby trap

86
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:16,020
would be to have rocks fall on someone.

87
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:18,680
And even though the Egyptians didn't do that very often,

88
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:21,380
we do have a couple of examples of it occurring.

89
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:28,620
A popular trap was the man burying booby trap.

90
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,360
A large funnel was hollowed out in the ceiling of the corridor

91
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:34,520
that led to the burial chamber.

92
00:05:34,620 --> 00:05:37,800
The funnel was then filled with rocks and sand.

93
00:05:37,900 --> 00:05:42,800
A large blocking stone was then rolled in under the shaft.

94
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:44,980
A robber trying to dig around the blocking stone

95
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:47,980
would trigger the sticks holding the funnel together,

96
00:05:47,981 --> 00:05:52,780
collapsing the entire thing and burying the thief alive.

97
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:55,580
In the tomb that we were working on a few years ago,

98
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,620
we ran across exactly the same thing.

99
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:01,720
And the remnants of that funnel actually did come down at one

100
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,740
point, and we had to evacuate the tomb very, very quickly.

101
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:10,840
Tomb builders also liked digging bottomless pits

102
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:14,140
hidden under camouflaged covers.

103
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:17,240
A single careless step would send unsuspecting intruders

104
00:06:17,241 --> 00:06:22,540
plummeting through a false floor and into a dark abyss.

105
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:24,280
The deeper ones of these, of course,

106
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:28,780
they would never be able to scale their way out of.

107
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,320
There's some evidence that suggests

108
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:33,220
poisonous powders were spread across the burial chamber's

109
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,920
floor.

110
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:36,920
When an intruder entered, the powders

111
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:41,240
were released into the air and inhaled.

112
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:44,640
In fact, a poisonous substance was unleashed in a tomb in Egypt's

113
00:06:44,641 --> 00:06:48,240
of the Golden Mummies in September 2000,

114
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:51,440
as archaeologist Zahi Hawass and his team

115
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:53,980
entered the main burial chamber.

116
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:57,180
Without realizing it, they stepped into a two-foot layer

117
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:59,380
of yellow powder, sending it airborne.

118
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:04,300
The team became ill almost instantly

119
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:06,800
and was forced to evacuate.

120
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:09,740
20 workers wearing gas masks labored for two weeks

121
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:11,940
to remove the mysterious substance,

122
00:07:11,941 --> 00:07:15,440
later identified as tainted iron oxide.

123
00:07:15,441 --> 00:07:19,440
But was it a booby trap?

124
00:07:19,441 --> 00:07:22,440
The only question, of course, is whether this material

125
00:07:22,441 --> 00:07:24,440
was put there by the Egyptians.

126
00:07:24,441 --> 00:07:27,440
There is indication that in some of the tombs,

127
00:07:27,441 --> 00:07:31,440
there is lethal bacteria and molds that have come on the

128
00:07:31,441 --> 00:07:32,440
walls.

129
00:07:32,441 --> 00:07:35,440
Aspergillus is one of them that has been recognized.

130
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:41,440
Over 4,000 miles east of Egypt's valley,

131
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:44,440
of the Golden Mummies, lay the ruins of one of the greatest

132
00:07:44,441 --> 00:07:48,440
discoveries of all time. It is here that archaeologists

133
00:07:48,441 --> 00:07:52,440
struggle with a far more deadly puzzle.

134
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:00,440
100,000 slaves toiled for 36 years to build the mausoleum of

135
00:08:00,441 --> 00:08:04,440
the Chinese emperor, Qin Shi Huang. It covered 10 square

136
00:08:04,441 --> 00:08:08,440
miles and was buried 15 stories underground.

137
00:08:08,441 --> 00:08:12,440
For two millennia, an army of 8,000 life-sized terracotta

138
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:17,880
soldiers and horses kept watch over his complex burial site.

139
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:21,380
According to folklore, scores of booby traps wait in silence

140
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:23,580
as well.

141
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:26,600
By popular myth and legend, the tomb

142
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:30,500
is protected by an elaborate system of crossbows

143
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:34,240
and other weapons waiting to be discharged

144
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:36,340
at any potential grave robber.

145
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:40,340
There are also supposed to be large ponds or even rivers of

146
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:46,320
mercury placed inside the tomb for its noxious fumes, which

147
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:51,820
would theoretically overcome any intruder.

148
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:55,320
Today, most of the site has yet to be excavated.

149
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:58,460
One of the reasons is that Chinese archaeologists and their

150
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:02,660
workers believe that crossbows do exist.

151
00:09:02,661 --> 00:09:06,460
They are proceeding slowly with great caution so as not to

152
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:09,200
become modern victims of the Chinese emperor's ancient

153
00:09:09,300 --> 00:09:10,200
booby traps.

154
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,500
Early engineers had managed to turn the art of protecting

155
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:20,820
burial grounds and tombs into a deadly science.

156
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:23,820
But it would take ancient armies and military geniuses

157
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:27,260
to transform booby traps from guardians of the dead

158
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:28,760
to weapons of war.

159
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:35,200
It was on the battlefields of North Africa, Greece,

160
00:09:35,201 --> 00:09:38,620
and the ancient Near East that military geniuses like Alexander

161
00:09:38,621 --> 00:09:41,900
the Great and Julius Caesar first discovered

162
00:09:41,901 --> 00:09:46,360
the tactical significance of booby traps.

163
00:09:46,361 --> 00:09:48,760
By setting up hidden snares and traps,

164
00:09:48,761 --> 00:09:51,820
they realized they could injure or kill unsuspecting enemies

165
00:09:51,821 --> 00:09:53,820
without exposing their own troops to danger.

166
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:59,600
Early on, commanders favored camouflaged pits,

167
00:09:59,601 --> 00:10:02,060
deep enough to hold razor sharp spears,

168
00:10:02,061 --> 00:10:05,020
angled to impale the foe.

169
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:08,500
The other thing was a cauldrop, which was a sharpened,

170
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:12,100
triangular piece of metal that could be thrown out in front

171
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:15,600
so that the enemy would run over it with their horses.

172
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:19,420
But it could also be used as an anti-infantry weapon.

173
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:22,220
Julius Caesar used cauldrops during the Battle of Alicia

174
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:25,020
in 52 BC.

175
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:27,620
As his legions seemed headed for defeat,

176
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:29,260
Caesar ordered hundreds of cauldrops

177
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:31,420
to be strategically positioned on the ground

178
00:10:31,421 --> 00:10:35,420
of his defenses. As wave after wave of the Gallic

179
00:10:35,421 --> 00:10:38,920
cavalry charged his line, his hidden booby traps tore at the

180
00:10:38,921 --> 00:10:41,920
hooves of the attacking army's horses, slowing down their

181
00:10:41,921 --> 00:10:45,920
assault. Military historians believe it

182
00:10:45,921 --> 00:10:48,920
was this strategy that helped Julius Caesar to turn a losing

183
00:10:48,921 --> 00:10:54,920
battle into a victory. Not surprisingly, today there

184
00:10:54,921 --> 00:10:57,920
is a modern version of the ancient cauldron.

185
00:10:57,921 --> 00:11:01,520
The road spikes used by police officers in pursuit of a suspect

186
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:03,960
fleeing in a vehicle.

187
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:07,960
The spikes are made in a certain way that they'll always be

188
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:11,460
upright, and when the bad guy runs over it and flattens all

189
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:13,860
four tires and hopefully stops the pursuit.

190
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:19,980
The invention of gunpowder in Asia during the 12th century

191
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,280
vastly increased the potential for carnage on the battlefield.

192
00:11:24,281 --> 00:11:27,880
As a result, gunpowder soon became a staple ingredient in

193
00:11:27,980 --> 00:11:31,680
the manufacture of booby traps.

194
00:11:31,780 --> 00:11:35,320
In Europe during the Middle Ages, warring kings and landowners

195
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:38,020
protected the length and breadth of their estates' boundaries

196
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,000
by laying tripwires connected to containers packed with

197
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:45,940
explosives, nails, and broken glass.

198
00:11:46,040 --> 00:11:49,580
During the American Civil War, it was soldiers on both sides

199
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:54,280
who took booby trapping to a new level of sophistication.

200
00:11:54,281 --> 00:11:59,160
We have a pistol barrel, a lock work off a regular percussion

201
00:11:59,161 --> 00:12:01,160
type weapon system, and a trigger.

202
00:12:01,161 --> 00:12:03,160
And as the triggering mechanism is disturbed,

203
00:12:03,161 --> 00:12:06,520
the safety bar would fall free, the locking mechanism would trip

204
00:12:06,521 --> 00:12:10,200
forward, and bang, they have their man.

205
00:12:10,201 --> 00:12:12,600
They connected their devices to everyday objects,

206
00:12:12,601 --> 00:12:15,400
like a spoon or a jackknife.

207
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:18,600
The items were left behind when they moved out of the area.

208
00:12:18,601 --> 00:12:22,200
The booby traps were literally hidden in plain sight.

209
00:12:22,201 --> 00:12:25,200
When the enemy entered the abandoned camp and picked up

210
00:12:25,201 --> 00:12:28,200
the harmless-looking spooner knife, the booby trap would

211
00:12:28,201 --> 00:12:35,200
detonate. By the time World War I began in

212
00:12:35,201 --> 00:12:39,200
1914, the machine gun and artillery were the dominant

213
00:12:39,201 --> 00:12:43,200
weapons on the battlefield. The use of this hardware made

214
00:12:43,201 --> 00:12:49,200
free movement impossible, so both sides adopted trench

215
00:12:49,201 --> 00:12:52,040
But as the American, French and British forces attempted

216
00:12:52,041 --> 00:12:55,040
to storm the German trenches, they found that yet another

217
00:12:55,041 --> 00:12:57,040
deadly weapon awaited them.

218
00:12:59,040 --> 00:13:02,040
The Germans got very good at booby-trapping devices,

219
00:13:02,041 --> 00:13:05,440
like tripwires across the entrances of dugouts,

220
00:13:05,441 --> 00:13:07,960
so that if you entered a dugout and you hit the tripwire,

221
00:13:07,961 --> 00:13:09,460
it would explode.

222
00:13:09,461 --> 00:13:11,960
They also got very good at booby-trapping a helmet

223
00:13:11,961 --> 00:13:15,860
or a Luger pistol, because everybody wants a souvenir.

224
00:13:15,861 --> 00:13:19,200
So these things were really good at the end of the day

225
00:13:19,201 --> 00:13:21,600
that if you move them, they would explode.

226
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:25,600
During World War II, the art and science

227
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:30,040
of the military booby trap exploded, literally and figuratively.

228
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:36,420
There is a combination of ways that a booby trap can be

229
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:37,920
triggered.

230
00:13:38,020 --> 00:13:41,760
One way is pressure, and then any pressure on this

231
00:13:41,860 --> 00:13:45,420
would crush the exploder, and it would go off.

232
00:13:45,421 --> 00:13:46,560
and it would go off.

233
00:13:46,561 --> 00:13:49,680
You could also rig this up as a trip wire,

234
00:13:49,681 --> 00:13:52,500
so that when you pull this, it would go off.

235
00:13:52,501 --> 00:13:55,800
You could make this also a tension release device,

236
00:13:55,801 --> 00:14:00,740
so that if you cut the wire, it would also go off.

237
00:14:00,741 --> 00:14:03,240
German soldiers planted booby traps along roads

238
00:14:03,241 --> 00:14:06,100
and in fields to catch the unsuspecting or the inquisitive.

239
00:14:09,380 --> 00:14:13,500
Allied soldiers landing on the beaches of Normandy on D-Day

240
00:14:13,501 --> 00:14:17,100
anti-tank barriers that were rigged to explode if moved.

241
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:24,340
As the Allies pushed farther inland,

242
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:26,920
they found that the Germans had laid tripwires attached to

243
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:31,180
explosives across doorways and staircases, or attached to doors,

244
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:34,420
cupboards, and windows. Soldiers don't go through

245
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:36,520
doors. They go through the windows.

246
00:14:36,620 --> 00:14:39,400
And you would smash a window in with your rifle butt.

247
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:43,500
And when you smashed it, it would set the explosive off.

248
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:48,500
In late 1944, during their drive across northwestern Europe,

249
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:52,740
Allied forces came across an abandoned German headquarters.

250
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:55,240
No mines or booby traps were found,

251
00:14:55,340 --> 00:14:58,240
but when a soldier urinated against the building wall,

252
00:14:58,340 --> 00:15:00,720
an electrical connection made by the urine

253
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:03,360
set off a booby trap that destroyed that building.

